Warnings go to stderr as each stage produces them and change nothing
else: the document is still written and the exit code is still 0, so a
warning cannot break a build used as a CI gate. All three cases are
here at once — an imported file's own `service`, an imported template
that the entry file can never name, and a declared network no service
references.

The generated document is checked by `warnings.out/`, which is what
holds the second half of this: warnings did not stop the build.

```console
$ hllc build web.hll --out docker-compose.yml
lib.hll:1:10: warning: template `defaults` is declared in an imported file and is not applied — `defaults` is only looked up in the entry file; give it an ordinary name and apply it with `with`
lib.hll:5:9: warning: service `db` is declared in an imported file and is not compiled — only the entry file's services are built
web.hll:3:9: warning: network `unused` is declared but no service references it, so it is not emitted — add it to a service's `networks [...]` list, or remove the declaration
docker-compose.yml

```
